1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,524
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science A,

2
00:00:05,524 --> 00:00:11,550
Unit 1, part one: topics one point one to
one point five. We'll write our first Java

3
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:15,424
programs and learn how variables and
expressions work.

4
00:00:16,597 --> 00:00:20,672
Every app, game, and website started as
text that a person typed.

5
00:00:21,122 --> 00:00:25,858
In AP Computer Science A, you'll learn to
write that text in Java.

6
00:00:26,308 --> 00:00:31,214
Let's start with three big ideas:
algorithms, programs, and compilers.

7
00:00:32,364 --> 00:00:36,610
An algorithm is a step by step process for
solving a problem.

8
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:41,902
For example, to find the largest number in
a list: remember the first number.

9
00:00:42,352 --> 00:00:46,832
Then check each number after it. If it's
bigger, remember that one instead.

10
00:00:47,282 --> 00:00:51,037
When you reach the end, the number you
remember is the largest.

11
00:00:52,187 --> 00:00:57,587
An algorithm is just an idea. To make a
computer follow it, we write a program in

12
00:00:57,587 --> 00:00:59,120
a programming language.

13
00:00:59,570 --> 00:01:04,200
Here is a complete Java program. When it
runs, it prints Hello, world.

14
00:01:05,350 --> 00:01:09,979
Every Java program lives inside a class.
This one is called Main.

15
00:01:10,429 --> 00:01:15,016
Execution starts at the main method. Every
Java program needs one.

16
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:21,695
Inside is one statement. System dot out
dot print l n displays a line of text.

17
00:01:22,145 --> 00:01:26,561
Notice the semicolon. In Java, every
statement ends with one.

18
00:01:27,711 --> 00:01:33,187
But a computer can't run Java directly. It
only understands low level machine

19
00:01:33,187 --> 00:01:34,111
instructions.

20
00:01:34,561 --> 00:01:39,638
So a program called a compiler checks your
code and translates it into bytecode.

21
00:01:40,088 --> 00:01:44,398
Then the Java Virtual Machine runs that
bytecode on your computer.

22
00:01:45,548 --> 00:01:48,300
When code goes wrong, there are three
kinds of errors.

23
00:01:48,750 --> 00:01:54,466
A syntax error breaks Java's rules, like a
missing semicolon. The compiler catches

24
00:01:54,466 --> 00:01:56,558
it, and the program won't run.

25
00:01:57,008 --> 00:02:00,250
A logic error runs fine, but gives the
wrong answer.

26
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:06,526
A run time error happens while the program
is running, like dividing by zero. Java

27
00:02:06,526 --> 00:02:08,231
calls this an exception.

28
00:02:09,381 --> 00:02:11,962
Your turn. What kind of error is in this
line?

29
00:02:12,412 --> 00:02:13,692
Pause the video now.

30
00:02:14,142 --> 00:02:19,471
There's no semicolon at the end, so it's a
syntax error. The compiler will catch it

31
00:02:19,471 --> 00:02:21,012
before the program runs.

32
00:02:21,462 --> 00:02:23,659
Next, we'll store data in variables.

33
00:02:24,809 --> 00:02:29,097
A game has to remember your score. A store
has to remember prices.

34
00:02:29,547 --> 00:02:32,214
Programs remember things using variables.

35
00:02:32,664 --> 00:02:37,743
In this lesson, you'll learn how Java
variables work, and the three primitive

36
00:02:37,743 --> 00:02:39,128
types on the AP exam.

37
00:02:40,278 --> 00:02:44,032
A variable is a named place in memory that
holds a value.

38
00:02:44,482 --> 00:02:50,436
Think of it as a labeled box. The label is
the variable's name. What's inside is its

39
00:02:50,436 --> 00:02:50,861
value.

40
00:02:51,311 --> 00:02:56,921
In Java, every variable also has a type,
which says what kind of value the box can

41
00:02:56,921 --> 00:02:57,263
hold.

42
00:02:58,413 --> 00:03:01,848
AP Computer Science A uses three primitive
types.

43
00:03:02,298 --> 00:03:06,052
int holds whole numbers, like forty two or
negative seven.

44
00:03:06,502 --> 00:03:10,428
double holds numbers with decimals, like
three point one four.

45
00:03:10,878 --> 00:03:14,526
boolean holds just two possible values:
true or false.

46
00:03:15,676 --> 00:03:20,540
To create a variable, write the type, then
the name. That's called a declaration.

47
00:03:20,990 --> 00:03:25,534
Give it a starting value with an equals
sign. That's called initializing.

48
00:03:25,984 --> 00:03:31,584
Here, score is an int set to zero, price
is a double set to four ninety nine, and

49
00:03:31,584 --> 00:03:33,728
done is a boolean set to false.

50
00:03:34,878 --> 00:03:37,032
Variable names follow a few rules.

51
00:03:37,482 --> 00:03:42,090
A name can use letters, digits,
underscores, and dollar signs, but it

52
00:03:42,090 --> 00:03:45,696
can't start with a digit, and it can't
contain spaces.

53
00:03:46,146 --> 00:03:50,412
Names are case sensitive, so score and
Score are different variables.

54
00:03:50,862 --> 00:03:56,919
And you can't use Java keywords, like int
or class. By convention, Java programmers

55
00:03:56,919 --> 00:04:00,057
write names in camel case, like high
score.

56
00:04:01,207 --> 00:04:04,556
Sometimes a value should never change,
like a tax rate.

57
00:04:05,006 --> 00:04:09,124
Add the keyword final, and Java won't let
anyone change it later.

58
00:04:09,574 --> 00:04:13,819
By convention, final constants are written
in all capital letters.

59
00:04:14,969 --> 00:04:18,041
Primitive types store the value directly
in the box.

60
00:04:18,491 --> 00:04:23,577
Other types, like String, are reference
types. Their box holds a reference, which

61
00:04:23,577 --> 00:04:26,214
points to an object stored somewhere else.

62
00:04:26,664 --> 00:04:29,778
We'll explore objects in detail later in
this unit.

63
00:04:30,928 --> 00:04:35,985
Your turn. Pick the best type for each
value. The number of students in a class.

64
00:04:35,985 --> 00:04:39,462
A student's average grade. Whether a user
is logged in.

65
00:04:39,912 --> 00:04:41,192
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:41,642 --> 00:04:46,399
Students is an int. Average grade is a
double. Logged in is a boolean.

67
00:04:46,849 --> 00:04:49,494
Next, we'll build expressions and print
output.

68
00:04:50,645 --> 00:04:53,546
A program that can't show results isn't
much use.

69
00:04:53,996 --> 00:04:59,004
In this lesson, you'll print output in
Java, and do math with expressions,

70
00:04:59,004 --> 00:05:02,658
including a few surprises that show up on
the AP exam.

71
00:05:03,808 --> 00:05:06,069
Java has two common ways to print.

72
00:05:06,519 --> 00:05:11,639
System dot out dot print l n prints its
value, then moves to a new line.

73
00:05:12,089 --> 00:05:18,320
System dot out dot print, without the l n,
stays on the same line. So the next output

74
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:20,153
continues right after it.

75
00:05:21,303 --> 00:05:24,567
Text inside double quotes is called a
string literal.

76
00:05:25,017 --> 00:05:30,020
To put special characters inside a string,
use an escape sequence, which starts with

77
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:30,734
a backslash.

78
00:05:31,184 --> 00:05:36,496
Backslash n starts a new line. Backslash
quote prints a double quote. And backslash

79
00:05:36,496 --> 00:05:38,800
backslash prints a single backslash.

80
00:05:39,950 --> 00:05:45,547
Java's arithmetic operators are plus,
minus, times, divide, and the remainder

81
00:05:45,547 --> 00:05:48,164
operator, written as a percent sign.

82
00:05:48,614 --> 00:05:53,770
Here's the big surprise. When you divide
two ints, Java throws away the decimal

83
00:05:53,770 --> 00:05:54,096
part.

84
00:05:54,546 --> 00:05:58,152
Seven divided by two is three, not three
point five.

85
00:05:58,602 --> 00:06:04,684
If either number is a double, you get the
decimal answer. Seven point zero divided

86
00:06:04,684 --> 00:06:06,687
by two is three point five.

87
00:06:07,837 --> 00:06:13,042
The percent sign gives the remainder after
division. It's called the mod operator.

88
00:06:13,492 --> 00:06:19,284
Seventeen mod five is two, because five
goes into seventeen three times, with two

89
00:06:19,284 --> 00:06:19,999
left over.

90
00:06:20,449 --> 00:06:25,718
A common use: a number is even exactly
when the number mod two equals zero.

91
00:06:26,868 --> 00:06:30,004
Java follows the usual order of
operations.

92
00:06:30,454 --> 00:06:36,409
Times, divide, and mod happen before plus
and minus. Operators at the same level go

93
00:06:36,409 --> 00:06:37,772
from left to right.

94
00:06:38,222 --> 00:06:44,170
So two plus three times four is fourteen,
not twenty. Use parentheses to change the

95
00:06:44,170 --> 00:06:44,600
order.

96
00:06:45,750 --> 00:06:50,273
One more thing to watch for. Dividing an
int by zero isn't allowed.

97
00:06:50,723 --> 00:06:55,736
The program compiles, but when it runs, it
crashes with an arithmetic exception.

98
00:06:56,886 --> 00:07:02,345
Your turn. What does this line print?
Seventeen divided by five, plus seventeen

99
00:07:02,345 --> 00:07:02,966
mod five.

100
00:07:03,416 --> 00:07:04,696
Pause the video now.

101
00:07:05,146 --> 00:07:11,789
Seventeen divided by five is three, using
integer division. Seventeen mod five is

102
00:07:11,789 --> 00:07:14,085
two. Three plus two is five.

103
00:07:14,535 --> 00:07:18,866
Next, we'll update variables with
assignment statements and read input.

104
00:07:20,015 --> 00:07:23,492
Variables are only useful if their values
can change.

105
00:07:23,942 --> 00:07:28,614
In this lesson, you'll learn how
assignment really works in Java, and how

106
00:07:28,614 --> 00:07:30,406
to read input from the user.

107
00:07:31,556 --> 00:07:35,716
In Java, the equals sign doesn't mean
equal. It means assign.

108
00:07:36,166 --> 00:07:41,061
Java first works out the value on the
right side. Then it stores that value in

109
00:07:41,061 --> 00:07:42,630
the variable on the left.

110
00:07:43,080 --> 00:07:45,235
The old value is replaced and gone.

111
00:07:46,385 --> 00:07:50,886
That's why this line makes sense in Java,
even though it's false in math.

112
00:07:51,336 --> 00:07:53,299
Score equals score plus ten.

113
00:07:53,749 --> 00:07:59,225
Java reads the current score, five, adds
ten to get fifteen, then stores fifteen

114
00:07:59,225 --> 00:08:00,320
back into score.

115
00:08:01,470 --> 00:08:05,118
Let's trace a few lines, like the AP exam
asks you to.

116
00:08:05,568 --> 00:08:08,128
a is three. b is a plus two, which is
five.

117
00:08:08,578 --> 00:08:11,031
Then a becomes b times two, which is ten.

118
00:08:11,481 --> 00:08:17,268
Notice that changing a later does not
change b. b is still five. Each assignment

119
00:08:17,268 --> 00:08:19,438
copies a value at that moment.

120
00:08:20,588 --> 00:08:24,343
You can only assign a value that matches
the variable's type.

121
00:08:24,793 --> 00:08:30,981
Putting an int into a double is fine. Java
widens it automatically, so five becomes

122
00:08:30,981 --> 00:08:32,174
five point zero.

123
00:08:32,624 --> 00:08:37,720
But putting a double into an int is a
compile error. You'd lose the decimal

124
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:42,815
part, so Java makes you ask for it
explicitly. We'll cover that in the next

125
00:08:42,815 --> 00:08:43,291
lesson.

126
00:08:44,441 --> 00:08:49,177
Programs often need input from the user.
One way is the Scanner class.

127
00:08:49,627 --> 00:08:55,263
First, import java dot util dot Scanner.
Then create a Scanner that reads from

128
00:08:55,263 --> 00:08:57,286
System dot in, the keyboard.

129
00:08:57,736 --> 00:09:03,251
next Int reads a whole number. next Double
reads a decimal. And next Line reads a

130
00:09:03,251 --> 00:09:04,477
full line of text.

131
00:09:05,627 --> 00:09:10,647
Here's a small program. It asks for your
age, reads it, and prints your age next

132
00:09:10,647 --> 00:09:10,960
year.

133
00:09:11,410 --> 00:09:14,973
If the user types sixteen, the program
prints seventeen.

134
00:09:16,123 --> 00:09:22,911
Your turn. Trace this code. x is four. y
is x times three. x is y minus x. What are

135
00:09:22,911 --> 00:09:24,464
x and y at the end?

136
00:09:24,914 --> 00:09:26,194
Pause the video now.

137
00:09:26,644 --> 00:09:32,604
y is twelve. Then x becomes twelve minus
four, which is eight. So x is eight, and y

138
00:09:32,604 --> 00:09:33,322
is twelve.

139
00:09:33,772 --> 00:09:36,460
Next, we'll convert between types with
casting.

140
00:09:37,610 --> 00:09:42,623
Sometimes you need a double to act like an
int, or an int to act like a double.

141
00:09:43,073 --> 00:09:47,919
That's called casting. In this lesson,
you'll also learn the limits of what an

142
00:09:47,919 --> 00:09:48,727
int can hold.

143
00:09:49,877 --> 00:09:53,866
To cast, write the type you want in
parentheses before the value.

144
00:09:54,316 --> 00:10:00,245
Int cast of seven point nine gives seven.
Java doesn't round. It just cuts off the

145
00:10:00,245 --> 00:10:01,185
decimal part.

146
00:10:01,635 --> 00:10:04,537
Double cast of seven gives seven point
zero.

147
00:10:05,687 --> 00:10:08,417
Casting is a great fix for integer
division.

148
00:10:08,867 --> 00:10:14,567
Seven divided by two is three. But cast
seven to a double first, and you get three

149
00:10:14,567 --> 00:10:15,331
point five.

150
00:10:15,781 --> 00:10:20,974
Watch out, though. If you put parentheses
around seven divided by two, the int

151
00:10:20,974 --> 00:10:25,701
division happens first. You get three,
then three point zero. Too late.

152
00:10:26,851 --> 00:10:29,859
Since casting cuts off decimals, how do
you round?

153
00:10:30,309 --> 00:10:33,552
For a positive number, add one half, then
cast to int.

154
00:10:34,002 --> 00:10:39,505
Two point seven plus one half is three
point two, which becomes three. Two point

155
00:10:39,505 --> 00:10:43,495
three plus one half is two point eight,
which becomes two.

156
00:10:43,945 --> 00:10:47,039
For a negative number, subtract one half
instead.

157
00:10:48,189 --> 00:10:51,047
An int uses thirty two bits, so it has
limits.

158
00:10:51,497 --> 00:10:57,524
The largest int is about two point one
billion. Java names it Integer dot MAX

159
00:10:57,524 --> 00:11:03,081
underscore VALUE. The smallest is about
negative two point one billion.

160
00:11:03,531 --> 00:11:09,332
Go past the limit, and the value wraps
around. MAX VALUE plus one becomes the

161
00:11:09,332 --> 00:11:13,174
most negative int. This is called integer
overflow.

162
00:11:14,324 --> 00:11:18,783
Doubles have a different problem. They
can't store every decimal exactly.

163
00:11:19,233 --> 00:11:25,552
Zero point one plus zero point two prints
zero point three zero zero zero, with a

164
00:11:25,552 --> 00:11:27,190
four at the very end.

165
00:11:27,640 --> 00:11:33,198
That tiny difference is called a round off
error. It's why programmers avoid checking

166
00:11:33,198 --> 00:11:35,683
whether two doubles are exactly equal.

167
00:11:36,833 --> 00:11:42,277
Your turn. What does this expression
evaluate to? Int cast of nine point nine

168
00:11:42,277 --> 00:11:45,601
nine, plus double cast of five, divided by
two.

169
00:11:46,051 --> 00:11:47,331
Pause the video now.

170
00:11:47,781 --> 00:11:54,296
Int cast of nine point nine nine is nine.
Double five divided by two is two point

171
00:11:54,296 --> 00:11:57,594
five. So the answer is eleven point five.

172
00:11:58,044 --> 00:12:01,841
Next time, we'll use shortcuts like plus
equals and plus plus.

173
00:12:02,991 --> 00:12:07,668
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

174
00:12:07,668 --> 00:12:08,943
miss the next lesson.

175
00:12:09,393 --> 00:12:10,289
See you there.

